<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziyad ibn Abihi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 622–673</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ziyad ibn Abihi, &quot;Ziyad son of his father&quot;, was an Arab administrator of uncertain parentage who rose under the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. Having served Ali, he was won over by the Umayyad caliph Mu'awiya I, who acknowledged him as his half-brother and made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and then of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the east, which he ruled firmly until his death in 673.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziyad-ibn-abihi.jpg" alt="Ziyad ibn Abihi" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Cplakidas (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_Dirham_in_the_name_of_Ziyad_ibn_Abi_Sufyan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
